<TopicPage slug="subjects/anthropology/intermediate/kinship-and-marriage-systems">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Kinship is <strong>how societies organise relatedness — descent, marriage, obligation —
    and it is anthropology's most technical, most comparative subject</strong>. The finding of
    150 years of study: the "family" is a variable design, not a fixed unit — every element
    (descent, marriage, residence) has working alternatives.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the operating system, descent, alliance, and the remix" widget="equationStep" config={kinCfg} />

  <h2>Concepts that travel</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Kin terms encode worldview:</strong> Hawaiian systems (all cousins = siblings) vs Sudanese systems (every relative distinct) — the vocabulary maps what the society thinks relation IS (the linguistics lesson's kinship vocabularies).</li>
    <li><strong>Bridewealth and dowry are flows:</strong> property moving with marriage (cattle in southern Africa; dowry in historical Eurasia) — Goody's comparative work linked them to land systems and gender structures.</li>
    <li><strong>The Nuer trick:</strong> woman–woman marriage (a woman as legal "father" to her brother's children) — showing kinship is a LEGAL system that can be manipulated by design (Evans-Pritchard, 1940).</li>
    <li><strong>Why it matters now:</strong> inheritance law, citizenship by descent, IVF parenthood disputes, and "family values" politics all run on kinship assumptions — comparative knowledge is civic equipment.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Morgan, Systems of Consanguinity (1871); Lévi-Strauss, The Elementary Structures of Kinship (1949); Evans-Pritchard, The Nuer (1940); Coontz, Marriage, a History (2005).</p>
</TopicPage>
